<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daylamites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dailamites, Deylamites, Daylamis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Daylamites were an Iranian people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>, the highlands south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, famed as tough infantry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. They resisted Arab rule for centuries and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the 9th and 10th centuries, often in its Zaydi form, through Alid missionaries. In the 10th century Daylamite dynasties, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p></div>
